   "id": "8961fbd6",
   "metadata": {
    "jp-MarkdownHeadingCollapsed": true
   },
   "source": [
    "# 1. Introduction"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b12bfcd7-4915-4c68-b30b-004875d1ecdd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This notebook addresses numerical methods for computing the generalized singular value decomposition (GSVD) of a matrix pair\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m_A\\times n},\\qquad B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m_B\\times n}.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "The desired factors satisfy\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "A=UCX^{-1},\\qquad B=VSX^{-1},\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $U$ and $V$ have orthonormal columns on the active subspaces, $X$ is nonsingular, and corresponding entries of $C$ and $S$ satisfy\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "c_i^2+s_i^2=1.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "Computationally, the central problem is to find right directions $x_i$ that organize the actions of **both** matrices at once.  \n",
    "The factors $C$ and $S$ then measure how strongly each matrix acts on the same direction, while $U$ and $V$ record the resulting directions in the two codomains."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "78ba61c4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The notebook develops\n",
    "  <ul style=\"line-height: 1.25;\">\n",
    "    <li>a generalized-eigenvalue method for the special case in which $B^TB$ is positive definite;</li>\n",
    "    <li>a QR/CS method for general full-column-rank pairs that avoids forming normal equations;</li>\n",
    "    <li>the LAPACK GSVD factorization returned by Julia's <code>svd(A,B)</code>;</li>\n",
    "    <li>the interpretation of generalized singular vectors and finite, zero, and infinite generalized\n",
    "    singular values;</li>\n",
    "    <li>rank-deficient pairs and common right nullspaces;</li>\n",
    "    <li>numerical checks for reconstruction, orthogonality, and normalization.</li>\n",
    "  </ul>\n",
    "\n",
    "The structure and geometric interpretation of the factors are developed in [**GSVD_intro.ipynb**](GSVD_intro.ipynb)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "40d5b797-66d6-4f65-9565-e2f77f2ef439",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Computational Roadmap"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c12a73b8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The notebook follows two routes to the same normalized factors:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "\\begin{array}{ccccc}\n",
    "(A^TA,B^TB)\n",
    "&\\xrightarrow{\\text{symmetric-definite GEP}}&\n",
    "(\\lambda_i,z_i)\n",
    "&\\xrightarrow{\\text{rescale}}&\n",
    "(C,S,X),\\\\[6pt]\n",
    "\\begin{pmatrix}A\\\\B\\end{pmatrix}\n",
    "&\\xrightarrow{\\text{thin QR}}&\n",
    "(Q_A,Q_B,R)\n",
    "&\\xrightarrow{\\text{CS decomposition}}&\n",
    "(U,V,C,S,X^{-1}).\n",
    "\\end{array}\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "Why use two routes?\n",
    "\n",
    "- The **generalized-eigenvalue route** gives the shortest path from the ratio $\\lVert Ax\\rVert/\\lVert Bx\\rVert$ to the generalized singular values.  \n",
    "It explains what the values mean, but it requires $B^TB$ to be positive definite and forms normal equations.\n",
    "- The **QR/CS route** starts from the stacked action of the two matrices.  \n",
    "It preserves that action with orthogonal transformations and avoids normal equations, making it the better model for a numerical algorithm.\n",
    "\n",
    "The first route supplies intuition. The second removes its principal numerical and structural restrictions.  \n",
    "LAPACK extends the orthogonal-transformation approach to rectangular and rank-deficient pairs.\n",
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "289a4546",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
