   "source": [
    "# 2. Full Column Rank Case: Generalized-Eigenvalue Reduction"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "gep-worked-example",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### A Running Example\n",
    "\n",
    "We use the same matrix pair throughout the four GEP steps. Both matrices are nonsingular, so $B^TB$ is positive definite and every generalized singular value is finite. Keeping one example in view makes it possible to see what each step adds to the computation.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "gep-example-data",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:48.992187Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:48.991729Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:51.295653Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:51.294374Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "A = np.array([[3.0, 2.0],\n",
    "              [1.0, 4.0]])\n",
    "B = np.array([[2.0, 0.5],\n",
    "              [0.1, 1.5]])\n",
    "\n",
    "l_show(\"A = \", A, L(r\",\\qquad B = \"), B)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "da9e0f33-62d6-4d07-95ac-18053a217ede",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2.1 Step 1: Turn Relative Action into a Generalized Eigenproblem\n",
    "\n",
    "The GSVD seeks right directions on which the actions of $A$ and $B$ can be compared.  \n",
    "The natural scalar measure for a nonzero vector $x$ is\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\\displaystyle{\n",
    "\\rho(x)=\\frac{\\lVert Ax\\rVert_2^2}{\\lVert Bx\\rVert_2^2}.\n",
    "}$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the [**generalized Rayleigh quotient**](GEP_intro.ipynb#2-the-generalized-rayleigh-quotient) for the symmetric matrix pair $(A^TA,B^TB)$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ed2c0788",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Idea:** Search for directions at which the relative action $\\rho(x)$ is stationary.  \n",
    "These directions do not change the ratio to first order under a small perturbation  \n",
    "and therefore provide intrinsic comparison directions for the pair.\n",
    "\n",
    "To do so, rewrite the quotient as\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\\displaystyle{\n",
    "\\rho(x)=\\frac{x^T A^T A x}{x^T B^T B x}.\n",
    "}$\n",
    "\n",
    "The stationary-direction calculation developed in [**GEP: Connection to Eigenvalues**](GEP_intro.ipynb#22-connection-to-eigenvalues) gives\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "A^T A\\ z_i=\\lambda_i B^T B\\ z_i.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "The assumption that $B$ has full column rank makes $B^TB$ positive definite, so this is the [**symmetric-definite GEP**](GEP_computation.ipynb#2-symmetric-definite-case). It can be solved with a Cholesky-based transformation without explicitly forming $(B^TB)^{-1}A^TA$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Outcome:** Along $z_i$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\\displaystyle{\n",
    "\\lambda_i\n",
    "=\\frac{\\lVert Az_i\\rVert_2^2}{\\lVert Bz_i\\rVert_2^2}.\n",
    "}$\n",
    "\n",
    "The generalized eigenvalue is therefore the square of the relative amplification. The corresponding generalized singular value will be $\\gamma_i=\\sqrt{\\lambda_i}$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "gep-step-1",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:51.299238Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:51.298769Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:51.462200Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:51.460784Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def form_gsvd_gram_pair(A, B):\n",
    "    # Form the symmetric pair whose Rayleigh quotient compares A and B.\n",
    "    A = np.asarray(A, dtype=float)\n",
    "    B = np.asarray(B, dtype=float)\n",
    "    if A.ndim != 2 or B.ndim != 2 or A.shape[1] != B.shape[1]:\n",
    "        raise ValueError(\"A and B must be matrices with the same number of columns\")\n",
    "    return A.T @ A, B.T @ B\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "G_A, G_B = form_gsvd_gram_pair(A, B)\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"G_A=A^T A = \"), G_A, L(r\",\\qquad G_B=B^T B = \"), G_B)\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"\\operatorname{eig}(G_B) \\approx \"),\n",
    "       np.round(np.linalg.eigvalsh(G_B)[None, :], 3))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "gep-step-1-observe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The two Gram matrices are symmetric. The positive eigenvalues of $G_B$ confirm that the symmetric-definite GEP is available for this example.  \n",
    "At this stage no GSVD vectors have been computed; the matrix pair has only been converted into a form whose Rayleigh quotient measures relative action."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7ba1fbf3-810a-4b18-ab57-20c03eccfe05",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2.2 Step 2: Choose a Useful Scale for Each Right Direction"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "636b98f2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The GEP identifies the direction of $z_i$, but eigenvectors have arbitrary scale. A GSVD needs a specific scale because the quantities $c_i$ and $s_i$ are defined by the actual norms of $Ax_i$ and $Bx_i$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea:** Begin with a normalization that the symmetric-definite eigensolver produces naturally.\n",
    "\n",
    "Because $B^TB$ is positive definite, the generalized eigenvectors can be chosen $B^TB$-orthonormal:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "Z^T B^T B Z=I.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the same weighted orthogonality developed in [**GEP: Variational Characterization**](GEP_intro.ipynb#23-variational-characterization-symmetricdefinite-case).  \n",
    "Combining it with the eigenvalue equations gives\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "Z^T A^T A Z=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_i).\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "Column by column,\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "\\lVert Bz_i\\rVert_2=1,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "\\lVert Az_i\\rVert_2^2=\\lambda_i.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Outcome:** The GEP has separated the directions and measured the two actions, but its normalization assigns unit size to the $B$ action alone.  \n",
    "$\\qquad$ The GSVD instead requires the *combined* squared action to equal one. A second scaling step is still needed."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "gep-step-2",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:51.465089Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:51.464926Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.618368Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.617142Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def solve_gsvd_gep(G_A, G_B, *, tol=None):\n",
    "    # Solve G_A Z = G_B Z Lambda with Z.T G_B Z = I.\n",
    "    if np.linalg.matrix_rank(G_B) < G_B.shape[0]:\n",
    "        raise np.linalg.LinAlgError(\"G_B must be positive definite\")\n",
    "    lam, Z = eigh(G_A, G_B)\n",
    "    scale = max(1.0, np.max(np.abs(lam)))\n",
    "    if tol is None:\n",
    "        tol = 100 * np.finfo(float).eps * scale\n",
    "    if np.min(lam) < -tol:\n",
    "        raise np.linalg.LinAlgError(\"a computed generalized eigenvalue is significantly negative\")\n",
    "    return np.maximum(lam, 0.0), Z\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "lam, Z = solve_gsvd_gep(G_A, G_B)\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"\\Lambda \\approx \"), np.diag(np.round(lam, 3)),\n",
    "       L(r\",\\qquad Z \\approx \"), np.round(Z, 3))\n",
    "diagnostic_table({\n",
    "    \"||Z^T G_B Z - I||\": np.linalg.norm(Z.T @ G_B @ Z - np.eye(Z.shape[1])),\n",
    "    \"||Z^T G_A Z - Lambda||\": np.linalg.norm(Z.T @ G_A @ Z - np.diag(lam)),\n",
    "}, title=\"Generalized eigenproblem checks\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "gep-step-2-observe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The residuals verify the two facts needed for the next step:  \n",
    "the columns of $Z$ are separated comparison directions, and their $B$-actions have unit norm.  \n",
    "The diagonal entries of $\\Lambda$ measure the corresponding squared $A$-actions.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1fa2b14a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2.3 Step 3: Convert the GEP Scale to the GSVD Scale\n",
    "\n",
    "For a GEP-normalized vector $z_i$, the combined squared action is\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "\\lVert Az_i\\rVert_2^2+\\lVert Bz_i\\rVert_2^2\n",
    "=\\lambda_i+1.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea:** Divide by the square root of this combined action so that the two contributions form a normalized pair.\n",
    "\n",
    "Define\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\\displaystyle{\n",
    "x_i=\\frac{z_i}{\\sqrt{1+\\lambda_i}}.\n",
    "}$\n",
    "\n",
    "Then\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\\displaystyle{\n",
    "\\lVert Ax_i\\rVert_2^2\n",
    "=\\frac{\\lambda_i}{1+\\lambda_i},\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "\\lVert Bx_i\\rVert_2^2\n",
    "=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda_i}\n",
    "}\\quad$\n",
    "and therefore $\\lVert Ax_i\\rVert^2+\\lVert Bx_i\\rVert^2=1$.\n",
    "\n",
    "These norms define\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\\displaystyle{\n",
    "c_i=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\lambda_i}{1+\\lambda_i}},\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "s_i=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+\\lambda_i}}.\n",
    "}$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Outcome:** The normalization and ratio now have the GSVD form:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\\displaystyle{\n",
    "c_i^2+s_i^2=1,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "\\gamma_i=\\frac{c_i}{s_i}=\\sqrt{\\lambda_i}.\n",
    "}$\n",
    "\n",
    "The vector direction has not changed; only its scale has been converted from the GEP convention to the GSVD convention.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "gep-step-3",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.622481Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.622240Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.758381Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.757362Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def rescale_gep_to_gsvd(lam, Z):\n",
    "    # Convert B-normalized GEP vectors to combined GSVD normalization.\n",
    "    s = 1.0 / np.sqrt(1.0 + lam)\n",
    "    c = np.sqrt(lam) * s\n",
    "    X = Z * s                       # scale column i by s[i]\n",
    "    return X, c, s\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "X_gep, c_gep, s_gep = rescale_gep_to_gsvd(lam, Z)\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"X \\approx \"), np.round(X_gep, 3),\n",
    "       L(r\",\\qquad C \\approx \"), np.diag(np.round(c_gep, 3)),\n",
    "       L(r\",\\qquad S \\approx \"), np.diag(np.round(s_gep, 3)))\n",
    "\n",
    "diagnostic_table({\n",
    "    \"||c^2 + s^2 - 1||\": np.linalg.norm(c_gep**2 + s_gep**2 - 1),\n",
    "    \"||X^T (A^T A + B^T B) X - I||\": np.linalg.norm(\n",
    "        X_gep.T @ (G_A + G_B) @ X_gep - np.eye(X_gep.shape[1])\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "}, title=\"GSVD normalization checks\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "gep-step-3-observe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The directions are unchanged, but their lengths now satisfy the GSVD convention.  \n",
    "The first residual checks the paired scalar normalization;  \n",
    "the second checks the same statement for the complete right-vector matrix.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3a6118f1-a9cf-47cf-b79a-52b4c6f87793",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2.4 Step 4: Recover the Left Directions and Assemble the Factors"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a2ad8217",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Collect the normalized right vectors in $X=[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$ and the paired magnitudes in $C=\\operatorname{diag}(c_i)$ and $S=\\operatorname{diag}(s_i)$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea:** Once the common right direction $x_i$ is known, apply each matrix to determine where that direction is sent in its own codomain.\n",
    "\n",
    "For nonzero paired values, normalize the two images:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\\displaystyle{\n",
    "u_i=\\frac{Ax_i}{c_i},\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "v_i=\\frac{Bx_i}{s_i}.\n",
    "}$\n",
    "\n",
    "Consequently,\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "Ax_i=c_i u_i,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "Bx_i=s_i v_i.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "Doing this for every column gives $AX=UC$ and $BX=VS$, or equivalently\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "A=UCX^{-1},\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "B=VSX^{-1}.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Outcome:** The factorization separates three kinds of information:\n",
    "\n",
    "- $X$ contains common right directions;\n",
    "- $C$ and $S$ contain their paired magnitudes;\n",
    "- $U$ and $V$ contain the corresponding output directions.\n",
    "\n",
    "The matrix identities\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "X^T(A^TA+B^TB)X=I,\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "X^TA^TAX=C^TC,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "X^TB^TBX=S^TS\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "summarize the derivation and provide direct checks on the computed factors."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "gep-step-4",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.760510Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.760258Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.806532Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.804946Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def recover_gsvd_left_factors(A, B, X, c, s, *, tol=None):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Recover left vectors for active cosine and sine components.\n",
    "\n",
    "    A zero paired magnitude does not determine a left vector. Its column is\n",
    "    therefore left at zero rather than being given an arbitrary orthogonal\n",
    "    completion. Only the active columns are part of the compact factors.\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    if tol is None:\n",
    "        tol = 100 * np.finfo(float).eps * max(1, X.shape[1])\n",
    "    U = np.zeros((A.shape[0], X.shape[1]))\n",
    "    V = np.zeros((B.shape[0], X.shape[1]))\n",
    "    active_c = c > tol\n",
    "    active_s = s > tol\n",
    "    U[:, active_c] = (A @ X[:, active_c]) / c[active_c]\n",
    "    V[:, active_s] = (B @ X[:, active_s]) / s[active_s]\n",
    "    return U, V\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "U_gep, V_gep = recover_gsvd_left_factors(A, B, X_gep, c_gep, s_gep)\n",
    "active_c_gep = c_gep > 100 * np.finfo(float).eps * max(1, X_gep.shape[1])\n",
    "active_s_gep = s_gep > 100 * np.finfo(float).eps * max(1, X_gep.shape[1])\n",
    "U_active = U_gep[:, active_c_gep]\n",
    "V_active = V_gep[:, active_s_gep]\n",
    "\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"U \\approx \"), np.round(U_gep, 3),\n",
    "       L(r\",\\qquad V \\approx \"), np.round(V_gep, 3))\n",
    "diagnostic_table({\n",
    "    \"||A X - U C||\": np.linalg.norm(A @ X_gep - U_gep @ np.diag(c_gep)),\n",
    "    \"||B X - V S||\": np.linalg.norm(B @ X_gep - V_gep @ np.diag(s_gep)),\n",
    "    \"||U_active^T U_active - I||\": np.linalg.norm(\n",
    "        U_active.T @ U_active - np.eye(U_active.shape[1])\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    \"||V_active^T V_active - I||\": np.linalg.norm(\n",
    "        V_active.T @ V_active - np.eye(V_active.shape[1])\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "}, title=\"Recovered active left-factor checks\")\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "gep-step-4-observe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The action residuals verify $AX=UC$ and $BX=VS$. The orthogonality residuals verify that normalizing the two images produced valid left factors.  \n",
    "The four step functions now contain every operation needed by the complete GEP-based reference routine.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "If $c_i=0$ or $s_i=0$, the corresponding image is zero and does not determine a left direction. The compact reference code leaves that inactive column at zero and checks orthogonality only among active columns; it does not construct an arbitrary orthogonal completion.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cd98395d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Remark:** explicitly forming $A^TA$ and $B^TB$ should be avoided in a production GSVD algorithm.  \n",
    "$\\qquad$ Normal equations can square condition numbers and reduce relative accuracy.  \n",
    "$\\qquad$ The reduction remains useful for interpretation, verification, and well-conditioned examples."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cc007246-4857-482a-941e-0283d5110cdf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### **Algorithm Summary**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5db8bfa8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The preceding derivation translates into the following operations:\n",
    "\n",
    "| step | idea | numerical operation | result |\n",
    "|:---:|:---|:---|:---|\n",
    "| 1 | Find directions with stationary relative action | Form $G_A=A^TA$, $G_B=B^TB$ and solve $G_AZ=G_BZ\\Lambda$ | $\\lambda_i=\\gamma_i^2$ and GEP-normalized directions $z_i$ |\n",
    "| 2 | Convert from $B$-normalization to combined normalization | Set $x_i=z_i/\\sqrt{1+\\lambda_i}$ | $\\lVert Ax_i\\rVert^2+\\lVert Bx_i\\rVert^2=1$ |\n",
    "| 3 | Separate the two contributions | Set $c_i=\\sqrt{\\lambda_i/(1+\\lambda_i)}$ and $s_i=1/\\sqrt{1+\\lambda_i}$ | $c_i^2+s_i^2=1$ |\n",
    "| 4 | Recover directions in the two codomains | Set $u_i=Ax_i/c_i$ and $v_i=Bx_i/s_i$ when denominators are nonzero | $AX=UC$ and $BX=VS$ |\n",
    "| 5 | Confirm that the reduction preserved the problem | Check reconstruction, orthogonality, and normalization residuals | A validated reference factorization |\n",
    "\n",
    "The code below follows these rows in order. It is intentionally explicit so that each numerical operation can be matched to the mathematical purpose above.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "43956127",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### **Example Implementation and Test**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "3c886283",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.813825Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.813685Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.822514Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.821075Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def _relative_residual(error, reference):\n",
    "    # Scale an error norm without dividing by zero for a zero reference.\n",
    "    denominator = max(np.linalg.norm(reference), np.finfo(float).tiny)\n",
    "    return np.linalg.norm(error) / denominator\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def _component_tolerance(n, tol=None):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Return an absolute threshold for normalized GSVD components.\"\"\"\n",
    "    if tol is not None:\n",
    "        return tol\n",
    "    return 100 * np.finfo(float).eps * max(1, n)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def gsvd_via_gep(A, B, *, eigenvalue_tol=None, component_tol=None):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Compose the demonstrated GEP steps into a compact reference GSVD.\n",
    "\n",
    "    Left-factor columns associated with zero paired magnitudes remain zero;\n",
    "    they are not arbitrary orthogonal completions.\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    G_A, G_B = form_gsvd_gram_pair(A, B)\n",
    "    lam, Z = solve_gsvd_gep(G_A, G_B, tol=eigenvalue_tol)\n",
    "    X, c, s = rescale_gep_to_gsvd(lam, Z)\n",
    "    component_tol = _component_tolerance(X.shape[1], component_tol)\n",
    "    U, V = recover_gsvd_left_factors(A, B, X, c, s, tol=component_tol)\n",
    "    return {\n",
    "        \"U\": U,\n",
    "        \"V\": V,\n",
    "        \"c\": c,\n",
    "        \"s\": s,\n",
    "        \"X\": X,\n",
    "        \"lambda\": lam,\n",
    "        \"active_c\": c > component_tol,\n",
    "        \"active_s\": s > component_tol,\n",
    "    }\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def gep_gsvd_errors(A, B, factors):\n",
    "    U, V = factors[\"U\"], factors[\"V\"]\n",
    "    c, s, X = factors[\"c\"], factors[\"s\"], factors[\"X\"]\n",
    "    U_active = U[:, factors[\"active_c\"]]\n",
    "    V_active = V[:, factors[\"active_s\"]]\n",
    "    return {\n",
    "        \"A action\": _relative_residual(A @ X - U @ np.diag(c), A),\n",
    "        \"B action\": _relative_residual(B @ X - V @ np.diag(s), B),\n",
    "        \"U active orthogonality\": np.linalg.norm(\n",
    "            U_active.T @ U_active - np.eye(U_active.shape[1])\n",
    "        ),\n",
    "        \"V active orthogonality\": np.linalg.norm(\n",
    "            V_active.T @ V_active - np.eye(V_active.shape[1])\n",
    "        ),\n",
    "        \"CS normalization\": np.linalg.norm(c**2 + s**2 - 1),\n",
    "        \"combined metric\": np.linalg.norm(\n",
    "            X.T @ (A.T @ A + B.T @ B) @ X - np.eye(A.shape[1])\n",
    "        ),\n",
    "    }\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "a41f2432",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.825290Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.825090Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.867325Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.866269Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "gep_factors = gsvd_via_gep(A, B)\n",
    "\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"C \\approx \"), np.diag(np.round(gep_factors[\"c\"], 3)),\n",
    "       L(r\",\\qquad S \\approx \"), np.diag(np.round(gep_factors[\"s\"], 3)))\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"\\text{generalized\\ eigenvalues\\ }\\lambda \\approx \"),\n",
    "       np.round(gep_factors[\"lambda\"][None, :], 3))\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"\\text{generalized\\ singular\\ values\\ }c/s \\approx \"),\n",
    "       np.round((gep_factors[\"c\"] / gep_factors[\"s\"])[None, :], 3))\n",
    "\n",
    "diagnostic_table(gep_gsvd_errors(A, B, gep_factors),\n",
    "                 title=\"GEP-route residuals and structural errors\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "21d24251",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Observe:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- The generalized singular values satisfy $\\gamma_i^2=\\lambda_i$.\n",
    "- The scaled right vectors are orthonormal in the combined metric $A^TA+B^TB$.\n",
    "- The action residuals and normalization error are at the level of floating-point roundoff.\n",
    "- This route requires $B^TB$ to be positive definite and is less stable  \n",
    "  than an orthogonal-transformation method for ill-conditioned data."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a6a0b3d3-2fa0-4b2a-8a78-ebd3cf27d81f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### **Why the GEP Route Is Not General**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6f25480e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The GEP route depends on $B^TB$ being positive definite.  \n",
    "If $B$ is rank deficient, $B^TB$ is singular and the symmetric-definite eigensolver used above is not applicable.  \n",
    "This is the same obstruction illustrated in [**GEP: Example with Singular** $\\mathbf{B}$](GEP_intro.ipynb#222-example-b-singular).\n",
    "\n",
    "The GSVD can nevertheless remain well defined because $A$ may act nontrivially on every direction lost by $B$.  \n",
    "Such a direction has\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "Bx=0,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "Ax\\ne0,\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "and must be retained as a pure-$A$ direction with an infinite generalized singular value.  \n",
    "This parallels the infinite eigenvalues discussed in [**GEP: Detecting** $\\mathbf{\\lambda=\\infty}$](GEP_computation.ipynb#4-detecting-lambdainfty-in-practice).\n",
    "\n",
    "The important condition is therefore not that $B$ have full column rank, but that the stacked pair identify the right directions needed by the problem.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "310f0a1d",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.869230Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.869077Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.908826Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.907246Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "A_singular_B = np.eye(3)\n",
    "B_singular = np.diag([2.0, 1.0, 0.0])\n",
    "\n",
    "try:\n",
    "    gsvd_via_gep(A_singular_B, B_singular)\n",
    "    gep_status = \"accepted\"\n",
    "except np.linalg.LinAlgError as error:\n",
    "    gep_status = f\"rejected: {error}\"\n",
    "\n",
    "diagnostic_table({\n",
    "    \"rank(B)\": np.linalg.matrix_rank(B_singular),\n",
    "    \"rank([A; B])\": np.linalg.matrix_rank(np.vstack((A_singular_B, B_singular))),\n",
    "    \"GEP route\": gep_status,\n",
    "}, title=\"Singular-B example\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e0539d62",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The zero diagonal entry of $B$ corresponds to a pure-$A$ direction with an infinite generalized singular value.  \n",
    "A general GSVD algorithm must preserve that direction rather than require $B^T B$ to be invertible.\n",
    "\n",
    "<details>\n",
    "<summary><strong>Check your understanding:\n",
    "Why can the stacked pair have full column rank even though $B$ does not?</strong></summary>\n",
    "\n",
    "**Answer.** The null direction of $B$ need not be a null direction of $A=I$. Therefore no nonzero vector is annihilated by both matrices.\n",
    "</details>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "85d41cac",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 3. General Case"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9f137e97-3db9-45f8-8c17-42e1fffb13c8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The GEP construction exposed two limitations:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. It treats $B$ as the reference metric, so it fails when $B^TB$ is singular even if $A$ supplies the missing information.\n",
    "2. It forms normal equations, which square condition numbers and reduce relative accuracy."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0e1a06b9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The general algorithm needs a different organizing Idea:\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea:** Work directly with $A$ and $B$ through orthogonal transformations. Orthogonal transformations preserve norms, do not require inversion, and can reveal rank without forming $A^TA$ or $B^TB$.\n",
    "\n",
    "First combine the two actions in a stacked matrix and use QR to isolate a common right factor. Then apply a CS decomposition to the orthonormal factor to separate the paired $A$ and $B$ magnitudes.\n",
    "\n",
    "The use of orthogonal reductions follows the same numerical principle as the [**Householder-based QR and RQ reductions**](GEP_computation.ipynb#32-the-a-rq-and-the-a-qr-factorizations) used for the general eigenvalue problem. Here, however, the target structure is a cosine,sine pair rather than a Hessenberg triangular pencil."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7cd7d4d8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3.1 Matrices in Generalized Singular Value Form"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9dde8bb6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The compact notation $A=UCX^{-1}$ and $B=VSX^{-1}$ suppresses dimension-dependent zero and identity blocks.  \n",
    "For a full-column-rank pair with $m_A,m_B\\ge n$, one may take\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "U\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m_A\\times n},\\quad\n",
    "V\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m_B\\times n},\\quad\n",
    "C,S,X\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times n}.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "When either matrix has fewer than $n$ rows or the stacked pair is rank deficient,  \n",
    "software commonly stores economy-size orthogonal factors and structured rectangular $D_1,D_2$ blocks.  \n",
    "Identities such as $C^TC+S^TS=I$ apply to the active right subspace, not automatically to padded zero columns."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bdd20c40",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div style=\"background-color:#F2F5A9;color:black;padding:0.5em;\">\n",
    "\n",
    "**Definition:** A matrix pair $(C,S)$ is in normalized generalized singular value form when its active columns are diagonal or block-diagonal  \n",
    "$\\qquad$ in a common column ordering and\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "C^TC+S^TS=I\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "on the active right subspace.\n",
    "\n",
    "A GSVD transforms $(A,B)$ into such a pair:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "A=UCX^{-1},\\qquad B=VSX^{-1}.\n",
    "$\n",
    "</div>\n",
    "\n",
    "Each right direction belongs to one of four classes:\n",
    "\n",
    "| $c_i$ | $s_i$ | $\\gamma_i=c_i/s_i$ | interpretation |\n",
    "|---:|---:|---:|:---|\n",
    "| $1$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ | pure-$A$ direction |\n",
    "| $(0,1)$ | $(0,1)$ | finite and positive | mixed direction |\n",
    "| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | pure-$B$ direction |\n",
    "| $0$ | $0$ | undefined | common right-null direction |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4b87e378",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3.2 Orthogonal Reduction of a Full-Column-Rank Pair"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e4b18252",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3.2.1 QR Factorization of the Stacked Pair"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "qr-cs-worked-example",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We now apply the orthogonal-reduction route to the same matrices $A$ and $B$. The final generalized singular values should agree with the GEP result, but the intermediate quantities have different purposes: QR exposes complementary orthogonal blocks, and the CS step separates their paired actions.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "44c603cc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Step 1: Represent Both Actions in One Matrix\n",
    "\n",
    "The two matrices share a domain but generally have different codomains. Their right directions must therefore be transformed together.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea:** Stack the outputs so that a single matrix records everything the pair does to a right vector:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "K=\\begin{pmatrix}A\\\\B\\end{pmatrix}\n",
    "\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(m_A+m_B)\\times n}.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "For every $x$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "Kx=\\begin{pmatrix}Ax\\\\Bx\\end{pmatrix},\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "\\lVert Kx\\rVert_2^2=\\lVert Ax\\rVert_2^2+\\lVert Bx\\rVert_2^2.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "The stacked norm is exactly the combined norm used in the GSVD normalization. Stacking also makes the common nullspace visible:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "Kx=0\n",
    "\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n",
    "Ax=0\\ \\text{and}\\ Bx=0.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Outcome:** If $K$ has full column rank, every nonzero right direction is detected by at least one matrix. This is the appropriate full-rank assumption for the pair; neither $A$ nor $B$ must individually have full column rank.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "qr-cs-step-1",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.912409Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.912282Z",
