    "def stack_gsvd_pair(A, B):\n",
    "    # Represent the two maps as one map with a shared domain.\n",
    "    A = np.asarray(A, dtype=float)\n",
    "    B = np.asarray(B, dtype=float)\n",
    "    if A.ndim != 2 or B.ndim != 2 or A.shape[1] != B.shape[1]:\n",
    "        raise ValueError(\"A and B must be matrices with the same number of columns\")\n",
    "    return np.vstack((A, B))\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "K = stack_gsvd_pair(A, B)\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"K=\\begin{pmatrix}A\\\\B\\end{pmatrix} = \"), K)\n",
    "diagnostic_table({\n",
    "    \"rank(K)\": np.linalg.matrix_rank(K),\n",
    "    \"number of columns\": K.shape[1],\n",
    "}, title=\"Stacked-pair dimensions\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "qr-cs-step-1-observe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The rank check confirms that the pair has no common right-null direction. Notice that this test concerns the stack, not either matrix separately.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "33b4137e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Step 2: Separate Geometry from Common Right Scaling\n",
    "\n",
    "Compute the thin QR factorization\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "K=QR,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "Q^TQ=I.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea:** Replace the original stacked columns by an orthonormal basis for the same column space.  \n",
    "This removes scaling and correlation from the stacked columns without changing the subspace they span.\n",
    "\n",
    "Stable QR implementations use Householder reflections, as developed in [**GEP: Householder Reflections**](GEP_computation.ipynb#321-householder-reflections). The reflectors act on the left, preserve norms, and introduce zeros until the common right factor $R$ is triangular.\n",
    "\n",
    "Partition the orthonormal factor according to the original row blocks:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "Q=\\begin{pmatrix}Q_A\\\\Q_B\\end{pmatrix}.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "Then\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "A=Q_AR,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "B=Q_BR.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Outcome:** The orthonormal blocks $Q_A$ and $Q_B$ contain the relative geometry of the two actions. The nonsingular triangular factor $R$ contains their common right-coordinate scaling. Because the same $R$ appears in both equations, the shared-domain structure has been preserved.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "qr-cs-step-2",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.944824Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:52.944703Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.070287Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.069229Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def factor_stacked_pair(K, m_A):\n",
    "    # Compute thin QR and recover the row blocks associated with A and B.\n",
    "    Q, R = qr(K, mode=\"economic\")\n",
    "    if np.linalg.matrix_rank(R) < R.shape[1]:\n",
    "        raise np.linalg.LinAlgError(\"the stacked pair does not have full column rank\")\n",
    "    return Q[:m_A, :], Q[m_A:, :], R\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Q_A, Q_B, R = factor_stacked_pair(K, A.shape[0])\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"Q_A \\approx \"), np.round(Q_A, 3),\n",
    "       L(r\",\\qquad Q_B \\approx \"), np.round(Q_B, 3),\n",
    "       L(r\",\\qquad R \\approx \"), np.round(R, 3))\n",
    "diagnostic_table({\n",
    "    \"||A - Q_A R||\": np.linalg.norm(A - Q_A @ R),\n",
    "    \"||B - Q_B R||\": np.linalg.norm(B - Q_B @ R),\n",
    "}, title=\"Stacked QR checks\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "qr-cs-step-2-observe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The small reconstruction residuals show that both matrices retain the same right factor $R$. Everything distinguishing their two actions is now contained in $Q_A$ and $Q_B$.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "163be91b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Step 3: Identify the Complementary Structure\n",
    "\n",
    "The QR factorization has not yet diagonalized the two actions. Its value is that it creates an identity that makes a coordinated diagonalization possible.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea:** Use the orthonormality of the *whole* stacked factor to relate its two row blocks.\n",
    "\n",
    "Expand $Q^TQ=I$ using $Q=(Q_A^T,Q_B^T)^T$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "Q_A^TQ_A+Q_B^TQ_B=I.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "Q_B^TQ_B=I-Q_A^TQ_A.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "The two Gram matrices commute because one is a polynomial in the other. Hence they have a common orthonormal eigenbasis.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Outcome:** Diagonalizing the action of $Q_A$ automatically diagonalizes the squared action of $Q_B$ in the same right basis. The resulting paired diagonal entries must add to one. This is precisely the structure sought for $C^TC$ and $S^TS$.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "qr-cs-step-3",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.073602Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.073447Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.107046Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.105726Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def complementary_gram_matrices(Q_A, Q_B):\n",
    "    # Return the complementary Gram matrices and their identity residual.\n",
    "    H_A = Q_A.T @ Q_A\n",
    "    H_B = Q_B.T @ Q_B\n",
    "    residual = np.linalg.norm(H_A + H_B - np.eye(H_A.shape[0]))\n",
    "    return H_A, H_B, residual\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "H_A, H_B, complementary_error = complementary_gram_matrices(Q_A, Q_B)\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"Q_A^TQ_A \\approx \"), np.round(H_A, 3),\n",
    "       L(r\",\\qquad Q_B^TQ_B \\approx \"), np.round(H_B, 3))\n",
    "diagnostic_table({\n",
    "    \"||Q_A^T Q_A + Q_B^T Q_B - I||\": complementary_error,\n",
    "    \"||H_A H_B - H_B H_A||\": np.linalg.norm(H_A @ H_B - H_B @ H_A),\n",
    "}, title=\"Complementary Gram-matrix checks\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "qr-cs-step-3-observe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The first residual confirms complementarity. The second confirms that the Gram matrices commute, so one orthonormal right basis can diagonalize both. This observation is what makes the next SVD a joint reduction rather than an unrelated decomposition of $Q_A$.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "06a88058",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 3.2.2 CS Decomposition of the Orthogonal Factor"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "55c1baa0-c7bb-4889-b29a-d1f2175ce30b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Step 4: Find the Common Right Basis from the Upper Block"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c4b53915",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Compute an SVD of the upper block:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "Q_A=UCW^T,\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $C=\\operatorname{diag}(c_i)$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea:** Choose the right basis $W$ that separates the action of $Q_A$ into independent scalar actions.\n",
    "\n",
    "The SVD diagonalizes the upper-block Gram matrix:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "Q_A^TQ_A=WC^TCW^T.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus each $w_i$ is a right direction, $c_i=\\lVert Q_Aw_i\\rVert_2$, and $u_i=Q_Aw_i/c_i$ when $c_i\\ne0$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Outcome:** The $A$ contribution has been diagonalized. More importantly, the complementarity identity guarantees that the same $W$ also separates the $B$ contribution; a second unrelated choice of right singular vectors is neither needed nor allowed."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "qr-cs-step-4",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.109927Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.109771Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.236258Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.235074Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def diagonalize_upper_cs_block(Q_A):\n",
    "    # Use an SVD to obtain the common right basis and cosine values.\n",
    "    U, c, Wt = svd(Q_A, full_matrices=False)\n",
    "    return U, np.clip(c, 0.0, 1.0), Wt.T\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "U_cs, c_cs, W = diagonalize_upper_cs_block(Q_A)\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"U \\approx \"), np.round(U_cs, 3),\n",
    "       L(r\",\\qquad C \\approx \"), np.diag(np.round(c_cs, 3)),\n",
    "       L(r\",\\qquad W \\approx \"), np.round(W, 3))\n",
    "diagnostic_table({\n",
    "    \"||Q_A - U C W^T||\": np.linalg.norm(Q_A - U_cs @ np.diag(c_cs) @ W.T),\n",
    "    \"off-diagonal norm of W^T H_A W\": np.linalg.norm(\n",
    "        W.T @ H_A @ W - np.diag(c_cs**2)\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "}, title=\"Upper CS-block checks\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "qr-cs-step-4-observe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The SVD has found the right basis $W$ in which the upper-block action is diagonal. The second residual verifies that the squared cosine values are the eigenvalues of $Q_A^TQ_A$ in this basis.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6e116d7d-ed2d-4e7c-be47-a949c946c63f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Step 5: Infer the Paired Magnitudes of the Lower Block"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "eef08855",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Substitute the upper-block SVD into the complementary identity:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "(Q_BW)^T(Q_BW)=I-C^TC.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea:** Do not recompute the lower magnitudes independently. Determine them from the unit combined norm of each stacked direction.\n",
    "\n",
    "Define\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "S=\\operatorname{diag}\\left(\\sqrt{1-c_i^2}\\right).\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "Then\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "C^TC+S^TS=I,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "\\lVert Q_Bw_i\\rVert_2=s_i.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Outcome:** Each common right direction has a paired magnitude $(c_i,s_i)$ on the unit quarter circle. The terminology *cosine--sine decomposition* reflects this relation: one magnitude determines the other through $c_i^2+s_i^2=1$."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "qr-cs-step-5",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.237977Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.237838Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.284849Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.283295Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def infer_lower_cs_magnitudes(c):\n",
    "    # Infer sine values from the complementary cosine values.\n",
    "    c = np.clip(np.asarray(c, dtype=float), 0.0, 1.0)\n",
    "    return np.sqrt(np.maximum(0.0, (1.0 - c) * (1.0 + c)))\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "s_cs = infer_lower_cs_magnitudes(c_cs)\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"c \\approx \"), np.round(c_cs[None, :], 3),\n",
    "       L(r\",\\qquad s \\approx \"), np.round(s_cs[None, :], 3))\n",
    "diagnostic_table({\n",
    "    \"||c^2 + s^2 - 1||\": np.linalg.norm(c_cs**2 + s_cs**2 - 1),\n",
    "    \"||W^T H_B W - S^2||\": np.linalg.norm(W.T @ H_B @ W - np.diag(s_cs**2)),\n",
    "}, title=\"Complementary sine-value checks\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "qr-cs-step-5-observe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "No second SVD was necessary. Complementarity forced the lower-block magnitudes and the same matrix $W$ diagonalized the lower Gram matrix.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ef152f24-c48f-4f92-bfa4-fd1f8fcf27ee",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Step 6: Recover the Lower Left Directions"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b96e4719",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The values $s_i$ give the lengths of the lower-block images, but the corresponding directions are still needed.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea:** Normalize each nonzero image $Q_Bw_i$ exactly as in an ordinary SVD.\n",
    "\n",
    "When every $s_i$ is nonzero, set\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "V=Q_BWS^{-1}.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "Since $(Q_BW)^T(Q_BW)=S^TS$,\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "V^TV=I,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "Q_B=VSW^T.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Outcome:** The two blocks now share the same right basis:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "Q_A=UCW^T,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "Q_B=VSW^T.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "If $s_i=0$, the direction is pure $A$ and division by $s_i$ is invalid. A complete GSVD uses structured blocks to retain such directions. The reference implementation below assumes $s_i>0$ so that the central construction can be shown without obscuring it with case handling."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "qr-cs-step-6",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.288289Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.287983Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.324721Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.322566Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def recover_lower_cs_vectors(Q_B, W, s, *, tol=None):\n",
    "    # Normalize lower-block images to recover the columns of V.\n",
    "    if tol is None:\n",
    "        tol = _component_tolerance(len(s))\n",
    "    if np.any(s <= tol):\n",
    "        raise np.linalg.LinAlgError(\"this reference step requires all sine values to be nonzero\")\n",
    "    return (Q_B @ W) / s\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "V_cs = recover_lower_cs_vectors(Q_B, W, s_cs)\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"V \\approx \"), np.round(V_cs, 3))\n",
    "diagnostic_table({\n",
    "    \"||V^T V - I||\": np.linalg.norm(V_cs.T @ V_cs - np.eye(V_cs.shape[1])),\n",
    "    \"||Q_B - V S W^T||\": np.linalg.norm(Q_B - V_cs @ np.diag(s_cs) @ W.T),\n",
    "}, title=\"Lower CS-block checks\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "qr-cs-step-6-observe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The columns of $V$ are orthonormal, and the lower block now has the same right factor $W^T$ as the upper block. This completes the CS decomposition of the orthonormal stacked factor.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8439ddcc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Step 7: Return to the Original Matrix Pair\n",
    "\n",
    "The CS decomposition describes the orthonormal factor $Q$, not yet the original matrices. Restore the common scaling isolated by QR.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Idea:** Substitute the block decompositions into $A=Q_AR$ and $B=Q_BR$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How.** Using the same $W$ and $R$ in both equations gives\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "A=UCW^TR,\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "B=VSW^TR.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "Comparing with\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "A=UCX^{-1},\n",
    "\\qquad\n",
    "B=VSX^{-1}\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "identifies\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\qquad\n",
    "X^{-1}=W^TR.\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Outcome:** Every factor now has a computational origin:\n",
    "\n",
    "- $R$ restores the common scaling removed by stacked QR;\n",
    "- $W$ rotates to the common right basis found by the CS step;\n",
    "- $C$ and $S$ measure the paired actions;\n",
    "- $U$ and $V$ record the corresponding directions in the two codomains.\n",
    "\n",
    "This completes the full-column-rank QR/CS derivation.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "qr-cs-step-7",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
     "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.327250Z",
     "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.327121Z",
     "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.361003Z",
     "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-04T16:36:53.359836Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def assemble_qr_cs_factors(U, c, V, s, W, R):\n",
    "    # Restore the common QR scaling and package the compact GSVD factors.\n",
    "    return {\n",
    "        \"U\": U,\n",
    "        \"V\": V,\n",
    "        \"C\": np.diag(c),\n",
    "        \"S\": np.diag(s),\n",
    "        \"Xinv\": W.T @ R,\n",
    "    }\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "qr_csd_steps = assemble_qr_cs_factors(U_cs, c_cs, V_cs, s_cs, W, R)\n",
    "l_show(L(r\"X^{-1}=W^T R \\approx \"), np.round(qr_csd_steps[\"Xinv\"], 3))\n",
    "diagnostic_table({\n",
    "    \"||A - U C X^{-1}||\": np.linalg.norm(\n",
    "        A - qr_csd_steps[\"U\"] @ qr_csd_steps[\"C\"] @ qr_csd_steps[\"Xinv\"]\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "    \"||B - V S X^{-1}||\": np.linalg.norm(\n",
    "        B - qr_csd_steps[\"V\"] @ qr_csd_steps[\"S\"] @ qr_csd_steps[\"Xinv\"]\n",
    "    ),\n",
    "}, title=\"Assembled GSVD checks\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "qr-cs-step-7-observe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Restoring $R$ returns from the orthonormal stacked pair to the original matrices. The two reconstruction residuals verify the complete QR/CS factorization before it is packaged as one convenience routine.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ea01aae9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3.3 QR/CS Reference Algorithm"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "05ad2fba",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### **Algorithm Summary**\n",
    "\n",
    "Before reading the implementation, match each operation to the problem it solves:\n",
    "\n",
    "| step | problem to solve | idea | operation |\n",
    "|:---:|:---|:---|:---|\n",
    "| 1 | Preserve both matrix actions and expose common-null directions | Treat the pair as one map | Form $K=(A^T\\ B^T)^T$ |\n",
    "| 2 | Remove stacked-column scaling without normal equations | Replace the columns by an orthonormal basis | Compute thin QR, $K=QR$ |\n",
    "| 3 | Retain the original two codomains | Undo the vertical stacking at the level of $Q$ | Partition $Q=(Q_A^T\\ Q_B^T)^T$ |\n",
    "| 4 | Find common right directions | Diagonalize $Q_A^TQ_A$ | Compute $Q_A=UCW^T$ |\n",
    "| 5 | Determine the paired $B$ magnitudes | Use $Q_A^TQ_A+Q_B^TQ_B=I$ | Set $S=(I-C^TC)^{1/2}$ |\n",
    "| 6 | Recover the lower left directions | Normalize $Q_Bw_i$ by its length $s_i$ | Set $V=Q_BWS^{-1}$ when $s_i>0$ |\n",
    "| 7 | Return to the original pair | Restore the right scaling carried by $R$ | Set $X^{-1}=W^TR$ |\n",
    "| 8 | Detect implementation or interpretation errors | Test defining identities | Verify reconstruction, orthogonality, and $C^TC+S^TS=I$ |\n",
    "\n",
    "The reference routine below implements this restricted full-column-rank case with no pure-$A$ directions directly. It is a teaching implementation, not a replacement for LAPACK.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "898e2b6c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### **Reference Implementation**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "ea5f0658",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
